# exodusii Agent Bootstrap

This repository uses exodusii to read, write, compare, and query Exodus II finite-element databases.

## When to use exodusii

- Use exodusii to inspect Exodus/NetCDF simulation output (mesh metadata, blocks, sets, times, variables).
- Use exodusii to extract nodal, element, global, edge, face, and set variables at selected time steps.
- Use exodusii to compare results against a baseline with exodiff-style tolerances.
- Use exodusii for geometric region queries, element centers/volumes, region statistics, and region mass.
- Use exodusii to read decomposed parallel Exodus/Nemesis files as one joined database.
- Use exodusii to write or copy Exodus databases.

## Preferred First Commands

- `python -m exodusii learn`
- `python -m exodusii learn capabilities overview`
- `python -m exodusii learn capabilities commands`
- `python -m exodusii learn skills list`
- `python -m exodusii inspect FILE.exo`
- `python -m exodusii variables FILE.exo`

## Database-First Rule

- Run `python -m exodusii inspect` and `python -m exodusii variables` before guessing variable, block, or set names.
- Prefer the JSON commands (`query`, `stats`, `region-stats`, `blocks`, `sets`, `times`) over parsing `exoread`, `ncdump`, or other text output.
- Use `stats` for large arrays and `query` only for small extracted tables; `query` truncates by default.
- Use `python -m exodusii diff GOLD TEST` (or `exodusii.diff`) for baseline comparison instead of hand-rolled array checks.
- Prefer the modern API (`exodusii.ExodusFile`, `exodusii.ExodusWriter`, `exodusii.ParallelExodusFile`) in new code; use the legacy `ExodusIIFile` API only for existing callers.

## Self-Learning

- Use `python -m exodusii learn capabilities TOPIC` for compact capability retrieval (e.g. `query`, `python_api`, `mesh_geometry`, `region_reduce`).
- Use dotted paths for narrower answers, e.g. `python -m exodusii learn capabilities query.selectors`.
- Use `python -m exodusii learn skills SKILL` to read a skill, or `python -m exodusii learn skills SKILL .body` for just its body.
- Add `--terse` for compact JSON.

## Avoid

- Do not open Exodus files with raw `netCDF4`/`h5py` or `ncdump` when exodusii exposes the data.
- Do not guess variable names, block IDs, or time indices; discover them with `inspect`, `variables`, `blocks`, and `times`.
- Do not mix time conventions: the modern API uses zero-based time indices (`index:N`) while `step:N` and the legacy API are one-based.
- Do not dump full arrays into context; summarize with `stats` or limit `query` output.
- Do not treat the static capability dataset as a full API reference; source and API docs remain authoritative for exact signatures.

